  "final_text": "The dashboard and the rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. The rule's latest run finished with status `ok`.\n\n- **Fields:** the data view has these fields:\n  - `timestamp`\n  - `response.keyword` (the HTTP status as a string)\n  - `bytes`\n  - `clientip`\n  - `extension.keyword`\n  - `geo.src` and `geo.dest`\n  - `machine.os.keyword`\n  - `request.keyword` and `url.keyword`\n  - `referer`\n  - `tags.keyword`\n  - `memory` and `phpmemory`\n- **Dashboard:** \"EFF-0eb06281 Web logs overview\" (id `933c6411-ef44-4ccf-aec9-6bf2f7afd349`) has three metric panels: Total requests, Average bytes, and Unique clients (distinct `clientip`). Its default time range is the last 7 days.\n- **Rule:** \"EFF-0eb06281 5xx spike\" (id `6fdf4a07-8c70-4371-af84-1dc5f36e735d`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 documents with a `response.keyword` starting with \"5\" arrive in a 5-minute window. I didn't attach any actions, so a firing alert is recorded but doesn't notify anyone.\n\nThe first version of the rule had an unnecessary date-range clause in its query, and its first run failed on it. I updated the rule to remove the clause, and the run after that succeeded.",
